    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A small Allied army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a> holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> for three days, and the Allied fleet then defeats the Persian navy at Salamis</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> retreats to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> with a minor portion of his army and leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> in charge of his troops</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> march south again and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> is evacuated a second time, and the Spartans march out only after the Athenians threaten to accept Persian terms</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Summer 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> cross <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-cithaeron">Mount Cithaeron</a> and take positions opposite the Persian camp, and cavalry fighting leaves the Persian commander Masistius dead</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Persian cavalry cuts the Greek supply route and blocks the Gargaphian spring, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> retreat by night</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> pursues the retreating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and is killed, and the Greeks storm the Persian camp</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> after ten days and execute most of the pro-Persian leaders they find</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-plataea" data-v="3669b2b27d" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two lines face each other across the Asopos</b> The Persians lined up north of the river with their left wing facing the Spartans and Tegeans. Next to them stood the Medes and their neighbours, then the Boeotians and other Greek allies of Persia facing the Athenians.</li><li><b>The night retreat goes wrong</b> The Greek centre moved off late at night and went too far, ending at the Temple of Hera. The Spartans and Tegeans had not begun to move by daybreak, and a lochos under Amompharetus was left behind. The Athenians were blocked by the scattered centre.</li><li><b>Mardonius hurries after the Greeks</b> Mardonius learned of the retreat and rushed to Pausanias's position with the Persian left wing. The Persian cavalry attacked the Spartans and Tegeans, who had reached the Temple of Demeter, and the Athenians were engaged by the Boeotians.</li><li><b>The Tegeans and Spartans charge</b> The Tegeans ran at the Persian line as arrows fell, and once Pausanias had favourable omens the Spartans charged too. Artabazos brought up 40,000 men in a tight formation, slowly. Amompharetus's men had joined the Spartans.</li><li><b>Mardonius is killed and the Persian line breaks</b> Aeimnestus killed Mardonius with a stone. His guards broke and retreated and the rest of the army followed. The Athenians still fought the Thebans until they too retreated. Artabazos led his men away to Phocis, avoiding the camp.</li><li><b>The Greeks follow to the Persian camp</b> The Spartans reached the camp first and waited for the Athenians, who knew how to scale walls. The Tegeans were the first to breach them. The Persians could not form up and took heavy losses.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xerxes-i.jpg" alt="Xerxes I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Iran_Darafsh_(785).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darafsh, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>, after his accession in 486 BCE, prepared an invasion of Greece, including two pontoon bridges across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>. A congress of city states met, probably at Corinth, in 481 BCE and formed a confederate alliance generally called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. In August 480 BCE a small Allied army under the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a> blocked the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> for three days before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> outflanked it by a little-known mountain path. The Persians then burned and sacked Plataea and Thespiae, which had not surrendered, and took the evacuated city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Battle of Salamis</a> ended in an Allied victory, and Xerxes retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> with a minor portion of his army.</p><p>Xerxes left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> in charge of his troops, and Mardonius spent the winter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. He tried to win over the Athenians through Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, and when they refused the Persians marched south again and Athens was again evacuated. Mardonius repeated his offer of peace to the Athenian refugees on Salamis. Athens sent emissaries to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> demanding help and threatened to accept the Persian terms if it was not given. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> the Spartans were celebrating the festival of Hyacinthus and delayed ten days, until persuaded of the danger to all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. When the Athenian envoys delivered their ultimatum to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a>, they were told that the army had already left: the 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a> were at Oresthasium.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Plataea&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Andreas Konecny, The Battle of Plataiai 479 BC (Phoibos Verlag, 2022)</li><li>George Cawkwell, The Greek Wars: The Failure of Persia (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li><li>William Shepherd, Plataea 479 BC: The Most Glorious Victory Ever Seen (Osprey Publishing, 2012)</li><li>Paul Rahe, The Grand Strategy of Classical Sparta: The Persian Challenge (Yale University Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> the Spartans sent 45,000 men: 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a>, 5,000 other Lacedaemonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">perioeci</a>) and 35,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a>, regent for his cousin Pleistarchus, commanded the Spartans and chose Euryanax as his deputy, and the Athenians were under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a>. Ian Macgregor-Morris argues that the Spartiates were three fifths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s troops, while Hans van Wees argues they were all the citizens then fit to serve. Nicholas Sekunda argues that Herodotus inflated the allied army to 110,000 by assuming a lightly armed attendant for each hoplite; Andreas Konecny estimates 78,000 Greek soldiers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, Mantinea and Elis sent no troops.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> built a fortified camp of about 1.9 square kilometres on the north bank of the Asopos. John Francis Lazenby, comparing its size with Roman camps, calculates 70,000 men including 10,000 cavalry, and Peter Connolly 120,000, and most estimates lie in this range. Hans Delbrück took 75,000 as the upper limit including supply personnel and other non-combatants, and put the army including allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> at 40,000. Konecny estimates 44,000 to 66,000 infantry and 5,000 to 7,000 cavalry.</p><p>In Herodotus's arrangement the Persians faced the 11,500 Lacedaemonian and Tegean hoplites, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, Bactrians, Indians and Sacae stood in the centre, and the Boeotians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a>, Malians, Thessalians and Phocians faced 11,600 Athenians, Plataeans and Megarians. Thorax of Larissa and his brothers were with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Plataea&#8221; cites where it covers Greeks, Achaemenids, Composition and order of battle:</p><ul><li>William Shepherd, Plataea 479 BC: The Most Glorious Victory Ever Seen (Osprey Publishing, 2012)</li><li>Andreas Konecny, The Battle of Plataiai 479 BC (Phoibos Verlag, 2022)</li><li>Paul Rahe, The Grand Strategy of Classical Sparta: The Persian Challenge (Yale University Press, 2015)</li><li>George Cawkwell, The Greek Wars: The Failure of Persia (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> took positions on high ground on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-cithaeron">Mount Cithaeron</a>, about 4 kilometres across the Asopos from the Persian camp. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> began hit-and-run cavalry attacks. When cavalry under Masistius attacked the Megarians, 300 Athenians under Olympiodoros and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> took their place, and Masistius was killed after an archer hit his horse. The Greeks moved to a position nearer the Asopos. The armies then stayed in place for eight days; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says both sides had received bad omens from their seers. Mardonius sent cavalry against a Greek supply convoy and blocked the Gargaphian spring, the Greeks' only source of water.</p><p>After 12 days at the second position the Greeks decided to retreat by night to a place called the island, in front of Plataea. The retreat went awry. The centre advanced 1.8 kilometres past the island to the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, and the Spartans and Tegeans had not moved by daybreak because the Spartan commander Amompharetus refused to retreat, thinking it cowardly. The Athenians and Plataeans were blocked by the scattered centre.</p><p>Mardonius hurried with his left wing, about 5 kilometres, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a>'s line. Persian archers fired from behind a shield wall and Greek soldiers began to fall. Herodotus says Pausanias would not advance without good omens; the Tegeans ran at the Persian lines and Pausanias then ordered the Spartans to charge. Roel Konijnendijk argues that a soldier named Aeimnestus killed Mardonius with a thrown stone. The Persian guards broke, the rest followed, and the Athenians fought the Thebans, who eventually retreated.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Plataea&#8221; cites where it covers Greeks, Achaemenids, Composition and order of battle, Battle:</p><ul><li>Andreas Konecny, The Battle of Plataiai 479 BC (Phoibos Verlag, 2022)</li><li>William Shepherd, Plataea 479 BC: The Most Glorious Victory Ever Seen (Osprey Publishing, 2012)</li><li>Paul Rahe, The Grand Strategy of Classical Sparta: The Persian Challenge (Yale University Press, 2015)</li><li>George Cawkwell, The Greek Wars: The Failure of Persia (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> chased the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> to their camp. The Spartans arrived first and waited for the Athenians, who knew how to scale walls, and the Tegeans were the first to breach them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> estimates that only 3,000 Persians survived the fighting at the camp; Konecny argues that 3,000 may be the prisoners taken, while others fled. Artabazos led 40,000 men away to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, losing many soldiers to Thracian attacks, weariness and hunger. Herodotus says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale">Battle of Mycale</a> was fought on the same day.</p><p>The Spartan dead were buried in three graves, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> in the third. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> refused Lampon of Aegina's proposal to desecrate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a>'s corpse in revenge for the mutilation of Leonidas. One tenth of the Persian loot went to Pausanias and not to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, which concerned the Spartans.</p><p>After ten days the Greeks besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. The leading pro-Persian politician, Attaginus, escaped and his sons were pardoned, but the other medisers were executed without trial. According to Vitruvius, Caryae, which had medized, was captured, its men executed and its women enslaved. After Mycale the Athenians besieged Sestos, and the Persian general Artayctes was crucified there and his son stoned to death by Xanthippus.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Plataea&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath, Casualties:</p><ul><li>Andreas Konecny, The Battle of Plataiai 479 BC (Phoibos Verlag, 2022)</li><li>Michael A. Flower, Herodotus. Histories Book IX (Cambridge University Press, 2002)</li><li>William Shepherd, Plataea 479 BC: The Most Glorious Victory Ever Seen (Osprey Publishing, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus.jpg" alt="Diodorus Siculus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diodoro_siculo_-_storico_di_Agira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives the main account. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a>, a Greek physician at the court of Artaxerxes II, wrote one drawn largely from Persian sources. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> based his on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> cited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idomeneus">Idomeneus</a> of Lampsacus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a>. Herodotus says the Corinthians did not follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a>'s orders and took no part, while Simonides says they played a major part. Ray Nyland argues that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> Herodotus consulted had not fought in the battle, while the pro-Persian Greeks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> he consulted very probably had, and that he did not cite them because they were not considered reliable.</p><p>Casualty figures are uncertain. The Greek dead are 652 in Herodotus, 1,360 in Plutarch and 10,000 in Diodorus. Herodotus probably could not count the Spartan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">perioeci</a>. His 52 Athenian dead may be only the tribe Aiantis's memorial, as Cleidemus suggested, and Michael Flower and John Marincola argue he gave the Greek losses only for the final day.</p><p>Historians dispute the shape of the battle. Konijnendijk and Paul Bardunias argue that the picture of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> battle is contrary to the evidence, and that the Greeks formed a defensive line, sent out small groups to provoke the Persians and then pushed. Robert Gaebel argues that the cavalry covering the Persian retreat were Boeotian, led by Asopodoros. Rahe and Young give the date as 27 August; Rahe also allows the 28th.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Plataea&#8221; cites where it covers Accounts of individuals, Historiography, Strategy and technology, Formations:</p><ul><li>Andreas Konecny, The Battle of Plataiai 479 BC (Phoibos Verlag, 2022)</li><li>Michael A. Flower, Herodotus. Histories Book IX (Cambridge University Press, 2002)</li><li>George Cawkwell, The Greek Wars: The Failure of Persia (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li><li>John Francis Lazenby, The Defence of Greece, 490-479 B.C. (Aris &amp; Phillips, 1993)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpent-column">Serpent Column</a>, a bronze of intertwined snakes made from melted-down Persian armour, was erected at Delphi and listed the 31 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> that took part. Most of it survives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippodrome-of-constantinople">Hippodrome of Constantinople</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> sacrificed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> Eleutherios in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> of Plataea, where a marble altar was built, and the Athenians built the Stoa of Zeus in their agora. The Eleutheria, a festival of Zeus Eleutherios, included a race of 3 kilometres run in armour with a shield.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides of Ceos</a> wrote an encomium and an elegy on the battle. Marcello Lupi contends that the Spartans commissioned them, possibly through Pausanias, since they glorify both, and that in Spartan propaganda the victory avenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>. Christel Müller argues that Plataea became a place of memory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says some states built cenotaphs there; Müller argues they did so out of shame at not having taken part, while Lazenby argues these states had suffered casualties but could not recover their dead or had buried them in a collective grave.</p><p>George Cawkwell argues that the main lesson was the superiority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a> over Persian light infantry, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> afterwards relied on Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was lost to Persia through Plataea rather than Salamis. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> recognised that Plataea had paved the way for his conquest. Spoils funded buildings such as the Athenian Treasury at Delphi, and Caroline Vout argues that the victories at Plataea and Mycale heralded the Greek incorporation of Persian culture.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Plataea&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy:</p><ul><li>Michael Jung, Marathon und Plataiai: Zwei Perserschlachten als »lieux de mémoire« im antiken Griechenland (Vandenhoeck &amp; Ruprecht, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Plataea in August 479 BCE the Spartan-led Greek alliance defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a>'s Achaemenid army, and Mardonius died in the fighting. Estimates of the armies and the dead vary widely, and the battle ended the second Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a>, together with the naval defeat at Mycale.</em></p>

